{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And Moses heard, and it was pleasing, etc.</b> There is a need to know how such a straightforward halakhah — to distinguish between communal offerings and inaugural offerings — was hidden from him. And if he had some grounds to say that this distinction should not be made, why did he accept it from Aaron and not argue against it to refute it? Now, according to what I explained above, that even Moses needed to determine the matter — whether it was to be eaten or burned — and the thing was not conclusively decided for eating, his indignation at Aaron and his sons was on the side that he thought they had burned it without correct reason; or on the side that perhaps some disqualifying event had occurred to it, as our Sages said (Pesachim 62b, Zevachim 101a) — that he said to them: perhaps a disqualification occurred during the sprinkling of its blood, or it became impure, or it was offered outside its proper place — and all these things would be on account of a lack of vigilance on their part, and it was over this that he was indignant. But after Aaron stated before him the reasoning by which he judged and burned it, Moses accepted the reasoning and saw that he had not burned it except after judging a just judgment — and even if Aaron had not judged and Moses himself had judged, he too would have judged the matter and burned it, and nothing would have been withheld from him. And the verse's statement <b>and it was pleasing in his eyes</b> means — that which they did in burning it; and he was not indignant over the fact that they relied on themselves and did not ask him. Or it may intend <b>and it was pleasing in his eyes</b> — to Aaron, for he is good, and the doubled verb-forms indicate the doubled verb-forms." "<b>Or</b> it may intend <b>and Moses heard</b> — meaning, the Torah testifies that this distinction Moses heard from the mouth of HaKadosh Baruch Hu. <b>And it was pleasing in his eyes</b> — the thing that Aaron had done. And if you say: if he heard thus from the mouth of HaKadosh Baruch Hu, what place was there for his indignation? Perhaps it is because he had not yet communicated it to Aaron, and he feared that he judged it by a different reasoning that is incorrect and burned it. Or perhaps at that very moment this prophecy was stated to him, for prophecy was readily available to Moses, as they said (Numbers 9:8): ״Stand, and I will hear what HaShem will speak,״ and likewise here, immediately ״and Moses heard״ from the mouth of HaShem the judgment, <b>and it was pleasing in his eyes</b> that Aaron had acted justly in his judgment." "And our Sages said in the Midrash (Torat Kohanim ad loc.), and this is their language: Said Rav Yehudah — Chananyah ben Yehudah would expound all his days: difficult is the indignation that caused Moses to err; after his death I answer him regarding his words — for who caused him to be indignant? Rather, he erred. End quote. The reason for his saying ״all his days,״ and also Chananyah's reason for not stating it as Rav Yehudah did — which is the most obvious from the plain sense of the verse — and also Rav Yehudah's reason for not disagreeing until after his death — it would appear that Chananyah and Rav Yehudah each intend to speak well of Moses, for we have before us two paths: (1) the forgetting of what HaShem had told him, which is not characteristic of a faithful servant, (2) that he grew angry at the sons of Aaron and spoke harshly to them as mentioned, and also on Aaron's account he became angry in a hidden way. And Chananyah came and chose to attribute to Moses the failing of anger, in which he erred unintentionally, and that is the cause of the error — to say, heaven forbid, that he forgot the word of HaShem, which would be a great deficiency. And likewise we find in Sifrei (Matot 31:21) that Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah said: in three places Moses came to a state of anger and thereby came to a state of error, etc. — similar to the matter, ״And Moses was angry with Elazar״ etc. End quote. From here you learn that the matter over which he was indignant was not (on account of) error. And if you say: if so, why was he indignant if he did not err? Necessarily one must say that he was indignant over the fact that they ruled by their own judgment and relied on their own ruling without asking their teacher, and because of this the halakhah was hidden from him and he said ״you shall eat it,״ thinking that in absolute terms he was stating according to the law that it is fit for eating — not as I explained, that his words are not absolute but rather he is stating the opposing side to their actions. And Rav Yehudah came and disagreed, that one should not attribute to Moses indignation in absolute terms, for there is a side to say that indignation is worse than forgetting a matter, and it is possible to attribute that he erred and misjudged the law, and on account of his error he was indignant at the act that was done not according to his will, blessed be He — but without the error he would not have been indignant over his honor." "Now, this opinion was not decisive for Rav Yehudah but was balanced: there are grounds to be lenient regarding the aspect of indignation, and there are grounds to be lenient regarding the aspect of error. Therefore he did not disagree with Chananyah except after his death — for he stood by this exposition and did not state the other side that exists in the balance of reasoning, implying that the matter was decisive for him — that he was indignant, and in this he disagreed with him. Therefore he was precise in saying, ״after his death I answer.״ And in the version of the Sifra before us they said, ״I am as one answering regarding his words,״ and from the fact that he said ״as one answering״ the implication is — and not actually answering, and this too aligns with our words. Or it may be said that Rav Yehudah chose in absolute terms to state that Moses's reason was on account of error rather than to say that he was indignant, which is an improper act — for error is something a person can err in with distraction of mind, whereas indignation is an intentional act. And furthermore, if we say he erred, no stumbling block resulted from the error; but if we say he was indignant, a stumbling block resulted from his hand, for he was indignant and became angry. Therefore Rav Yehudah chose to say: who caused him to be indignant? Rather, he erred. And the reason he said ״after his death״ — to say that even all his days he expounded this, and even after the expounder had already died, I did not refrain from disagreeing, on account of the truth of the matter as it appears to me. Or he intended to say that he did not disagree with him during his lifetime and kept silent, but only after his death — for perhaps had he been alive, he would have answered the matter to prove his position. And our Sages — truth do the wise speak."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {2 "I inserted the negative לא into the parenthetical phrase 'לא (מצד) הטעות' as the sense requires; it reads literally 'not (on account of) the error.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:55:18.531562655Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}